 ## Background
-Visibility culling in volume rendering has evolved from simple geometric frustum tests to complex, view-dependent, and screen-space feedback mechanisms. Early methods focused on reducing the overhead of projecting and testing macro-cells, such as the screen regions method which divides image space into squares to skip full regions [S1, unofficial]. As datasets grew, view-dependent preprocessing like the 24-region decomposition allowed for more aggressive culling by exploiting directional visibility [S1, unofficial]. Modern techniques, particularly in interactive visualization and novel view synthesis (e.g., 3D Gaussian Splatting), integrate visibility culling directly into the training and rendering loops, using feedback from previous frames or contribution-based filters to manage performance and memory bandwidth [S1, unofficial].
+Visibility culling in volume rendering has evolved from simple geometric checks to complex, feedback-driven systems. Early methods focused on reducing the overhead of per-pixel visibility tests by grouping pixels into screen regions [S1, unofficial]. As datasets grew, preprocessing techniques like view-dependent visibility masks were introduced to statically cull invisible voxels based on viewing direction [S1, unofficial]. Modern approaches, particularly in interactive and neural rendering contexts, utilize dynamic feedback (e.g., screen-space subdivision) and probabilistic approximations to balance performance and visual fidelity [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
